WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:14.901
The next day was bright and sunny.

2
00:00:14.902 --> 00:00:20.435
The fourth graders went outside and collected autumn leaves for an art project.

3
00:00:20.436 --> 00:00:26.582
Ms. Barrow read a funny story about a lazy goose flying south for the winter.

4
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:29.919
Joy almost forgot about the broken violin.

5
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.443
Then it was time for social studies.

6
00:00:33.444 --> 00:00:38.245
"Yesterday we talked about goals," said Ms. Barrow.

7
00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:41.703
"Who remembers what a goal is?"

8
00:00:41.704 --> 00:00:46.155
"A goal is something a person wants," said Katie.

9
00:00:46.156 --> 00:00:50.631
"And something a person works for," said Olivia.

10
00:00:50.632 --> 00:00:56.355
"That's right," said Ms. Barrow. "Who can give me an example?"

11
00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:02.085
"If you want to do well on a test, you study hard," said Laura.

12
00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:07.802
"If you want to be good at baseball, you practice a lot," said Larry.

13
00:01:07.803 --> 00:01:12.519
"What if the thing you want costs money?" asked Joy.

14
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.023
"Like new shoes!" said Olivia.

15
00:01:16.024 --> 00:01:19.588
"Or a skateboard," said Luke.

16
00:01:19.589 --> 00:01:23.879
"We could call that a money goal," said Ms. Barrow.

17
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.372
"Do you have a money goal, Joy?"

18
00:01:27.373 --> 00:01:29.737
"Yes, I do," said Joy.

19
00:01:29.738 --> 00:01:33.246
"I want to play my great-grandma's fiddle.

20
00:01:33.247 --> 00:01:37.278
But the strings and bridge are broken and the bow needs repair.

21
00:01:37.279 --> 00:01:39.601
All that costs money."

22
00:01:39.602 --> 00:01:47.300
"Well, when you have a money goal, you must work to get the money you need!" said Ms. Barrow.

23
00:01:47.301 --> 00:01:51.919
"Me? But I'm too young to get a job," said Joy.

24
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.103
"There are other ways to earn money," said Ms. Barrow.

25
00:01:56.104 --> 00:01:58.048
"Just look around.

26
00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:02.053
Use your imagination. Think!"

27
00:02:02.054 --> 00:02:06.493
"Think," repeated Joy.

28
00:02:06.494 --> 00:02:09.077
Joy thought during science class.

29
00:02:09.078 --> 00:02:12.495
She thought on her way home from school.

30
00:02:12.496 --> 00:02:17.941
"Any ideas yet?" asked Katie as they leaned their bikes against the garage.

31
00:02:17.942 --> 00:02:23.313
"Nope," said Joy and kicked a walnut down the driveway.

32
00:02:23.314 --> 00:02:27.864
Joy was still thinking when the family sat down for dinner.

33
00:02:27.865 --> 00:02:32.645
Tonight they were having Grandpa's famous pork chops.

34
00:02:32.646 --> 00:02:37.668
"Dad, did you forget to wash your hands?" said Katie.

35
00:02:37.669 --> 00:02:42.329
Dad looked down at his hands. They were black.

36
00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.810
"Walnut stains," he said. "They won't wash off.

37
00:02:46.811 --> 00:02:51.178
We set up the walnut-buying station at the recycling center last week.

38
00:02:51.179 --> 00:02:53.832
I forgot to wear my gloves today."

39
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:57.614
"How is the walnut business?" asked Grandpa.

40
00:02:57.615 --> 00:02:59.751
"Busy!" said Dad.

41
00:02:59.752 --> 00:03:04.115
"Today Mr. Ferguson brought a truck full of walnuts from his place.

42
00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:06.979
He said he has never had so many walnuts.

43
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.765
We're going to have a big harvest this year."

44
00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:13.076
"What happens to the walnuts?" asked Katie.

45
00:03:13.077 --> 00:03:16.771
"And pass the pork chops, please."

46
00:03:16.772 --> 00:03:21.669
"Well, folks pick up walnuts on their land and bring them to the station.

47
00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:26.302
We put the walnuts in a big machine that rubs off the soft green hull.

48
00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:30.778
Then the nuts come out the other end of the machine and fall into bags.

49
00:03:30.779 --> 00:03:34.228
We weigh the bags and pay for the nuts."

50
00:03:34.229 --> 00:03:37.738
"How much are you paying?" asked Grandpa.

51
00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:41.262
"Fifteen cents for each pound," said Dad.

52
00:03:41.263 --> 00:03:44.104
"Fifteen cents!" said Grandpa.

53
00:03:44.105 --> 00:03:46.282
He shook his head.

54
00:03:46.283 --> 00:03:50.568
"When I was a boy, we were paid five cents for a pound.

55
00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:53.496
One year, I wanted to buy an old bicycle.

56
00:03:53.497 --> 00:03:56.812
I had to collect a lot of walnuts."

57
00:03:56.813 --> 00:04:00.891
"You had a money goal," said Katie.

58
00:04:00.892 --> 00:04:08.390
"I wonder how many boys and girls around here have bought their first bikes with walnut money," said Mom.

59
00:04:08.391 --> 00:04:13.457
"Who wants dessert?" said Grandpa. "I baked an apple—"

60
00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:15.919
"Wait a minute!" said Joy.

61
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.208
She turned to her dad.

62
00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:23.318
"Anybody can pick up walnuts and sell them at the nut-buying station?"

63
00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:26.324
"Sure," said Dad.

64
00:04:26.325 --> 00:04:29.060
"For fifteen cents a pound?"

65
00:04:29.061 --> 00:04:32.144
"That's right," said Dad.

66
00:04:32.145 --> 00:04:36.042
Joy's smile stretched from ear to ear.

67
00:04:36.043 --> 00:04:40.462
"How many pounds of walnuts are in our yard?" she asked.

68
00:04:40.463 --> 00:04:44.025
"Do you think there is one hundred dollars worth?"

69
00:04:44.026 --> 00:04:48.472
Dad laughed. "There might be!" he said.

70
00:04:48.473 --> 00:04:53.356
"But that's a lot of walnuts!"
